WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.668 --> 00:00:05.454
Squid game

00:00:05.454 --> 00:00:07.042
the breakout Korean series about

00:00:07.042 --> 00:00:08.479
players competing to the death

00:00:08.479 --> 00:00:10.821
for a giant piggybank full of cash

00:00:10.821 --> 00:00:13.219
is Netflix's biggest series launch,

00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:16.142
and the streamer's CEO Ted Sarandos says

00:00:16.142 --> 00:00:20.000
"There's a very good chance it will be our biggest show ever."

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.543
Critics have argued that the show

00:00:21.543 --> 00:00:24.894
offers a devastating critique of contemporary capitalism

00:00:24.894 --> 00:00:27.783
INSIDER: "This could be seen as a metaphor for capitalism..."

00:00:27.783 --> 00:00:29.783
"...the players represent the working class..."

00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:31.783
"...and the guards, the middle class..."

00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.255
In a Jacobin review headlined, 

00:00:34.255 --> 00:00:36.802
"Squid game is an Allegory of Capitalist Hell,"

00:00:36.802 --> 00:00:39.606
the writer asserts that Korea's extreme inequality is

00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:40.875
Squid Game's central theme. 

00:00:40.875 --> 00:00:45.645
A New York Times reporter wrote that is has "tapped a sense familiar to people in the United States

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:48.753
...that prosperity in nominally rich countries

00:00:48.753 --> 00:00:51.874
has become increasingly difficult to achieve

00:00:51.874 --> 00:00:53.874
as wealth disparities widen

00:00:53.874 --> 00:00:56.541
and home prices rise past affordable levels."

00:00:56.541 --> 00:01:00.405
And the show's creator told Variety that,

00:01:00.405 --> 00:01:04.046
"I wanted to write a story that was an allegory or fable about modern capitalist society...

00:01:04.046 --> 00:01:06.571
something that depicts an extreme competition

00:01:06.571 --> 00:01:09.426
somewhat like the extreme competition of life."

00:01:09.426 --> 00:01:12.507
Is there a theme more unifying in global pop culture than

00:01:12.507 --> 00:01:14.033
"Capitalism is bad?"

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:17.657
asks Vulture writer Roxanna Hadidi in her recap of one episode

00:01:17.657 --> 00:01:18.773
before continuing,

00:01:18.773 --> 00:01:21.218
"It helps that the statement is true, of course."

00:01:21.218 --> 00:01:24.248
But Squid Game has a much richer, and more resonant message

00:01:24.248 --> 00:01:26.201
than, "Capitalism Bad."

